\title{Univariate polynomial maps into a vector space}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{univariate polynomial map; vector coefficient; coefficient tuple; polynomial coefficient matrix}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Notation used in [OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), proof of Lemma 2.1; explanatory definition added on 9 October 2026.}

\p{Let #{W} be a real vector space of dimension #{N}. Here #{W} is the ambient space of polynomial values. Choose a basis #{e_1,\ldots,e_N}. A \em{univariate polynomial map} #{P:\mathbb R\to W} has one scalar polynomial for each coordinate of its value:}

##{P(t)=\sum_{j=1}^{N}p_j(t)e_j,\qquad p_j\in\mathbb R[\xi].}

\p{This definition is independent of the chosen basis: a change of coordinates takes real linear combinations of the scalar polynomials #{p_j}. For example, #{P(t)=(1+2t,3-t^2)} is a polynomial map from #{\mathbb R} to #{\mathbb R^2}. Its two coordinates are two scalar polynomial functions of the same variable.}

\p{Writing #{p_j(\xi)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}c_{jr}\xi^r} and collecting equal powers expresses the map in terms of its \em{vector coefficients} #{w_r}:}

##{P(\xi)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}w_r\xi^r,\qquad w_r=\sum_{j=1}^{N}c_{jr}e_j\in W.}

\p{The paper's notation #{W[\xi]} encodes these maps by their coefficient expansions. For a subspace #{Z\subseteq W}, #{Z[\xi]} consists of the maps with every #{w_r\in Z}, so their values also lie in #{Z}. For #{P\ne0}, its degree is the largest #{r} with #{w_r\ne0}; the zero map is included in every degree bound.}

\p{For degree at most #{h}, the \em{coefficient tuple} #{(w_0,\ldots,w_h)} belongs to #{W^{h+1}}. Slot #{r} corresponds to monomial #{\xi^r}. Thus #{W[\xi]_{\leq h}\cong W^{h+1}}. The \em{polynomial coefficient matrix} #{C=(c_{jr})} has #{N} rows, one for each coordinate function #{p_j}, and #{h+1} columns, one for each monomial #{1,\xi,\ldots,\xi^h}.}

##{[P(\zeta)]_{(e_1,\ldots,e_N)}=C\begin{pmatrix}1\\\zeta\\\vdots\\\zeta^h\end{pmatrix}.}

\p{The distinction is between the coordinate index #{j} of a value in #{W} and the degree index #{r} within each scalar polynomial #{p_j}. The monomial correspondence is with #{r}; the dimension #{N} of the value space and the polynomial degree bound #{h} are independent.}

\p{For example, with #{W=\mathbb R^2},}

##{P(\xi)=(1,3)+\xi(2,0)+\xi^2(0,-1)=(1+2\xi,\,3-\xi^2).}

\p{Its coefficient matrix has columns corresponding, in order, to #{1,\xi,\xi^2}:}

##{C=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0\\3&0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}

\p{This is a degree-two polynomial map #{\mathbb R\to\mathbb R^2}; for instance #{P(1)=(3,2)}.}

\p{In the [rank-sum application](cn-0027), #{W=\mathbb R^d\oplus\mathbb R^d}. A polynomial map has the form #{P(t)=(a(t),b(t))}, where #{a,b} are #{\mathbb R^d}-valued polynomial maps. Requiring #{P(t_i)} to lie in [the graph](cn-0026) of #{X_i} means #{b(t_i)=X_i a(t_i)}. This explains why evaluation in the subspace argument must produce a vector in #{W}.}
